Usage: zngur generate [OPTIONS] <PATH>

Arguments:
  <PATH>
          Path to the zng file

Options:
      --cpp-file <CPP_FILE>
          Path of the generated C++ file, if it is needed

          Default is {ZNG_FILE_PARENT}/generated.cpp

      --h-file <H_FILE>
          Path of the generated header file

          Default is {ZNG_FILE_PARENT}/generated.h

      --rs-file <RS_FILE>
          Path of the generated Rust file

          Default is {ZNG_FILE_PARENT}/src/generated.rs

      --depfile <DEPFILE>
          Path of the dependency file (.d file) to generate

          The dependency file lists all .zng files that were processed. This can be used by build systems to detect when regeneration is needed.

      --mangling-base <MANGLING_BASE>
          A unique string which is included in zngur symbols to prevent duplicate symbols in linker

          Default is "rust"

  -C, --cfg <RUST_CFG>
          A rust config value of the form key(=value1(,value2 ...)) to use when generating the zngur spec. i.e.  -C target_os=linux -C target_feature=sse,sse2 -C debug_assertions

          see https://doc.rust-lang.org/reference/conditional-compilation.html for possible values

          combined with any values loaded from rustc (if enabled)

          Default is an empty configuration

  -F, --feature <RUST_FEATURES>
          A feature name to enable when generating the zngur spec

          combined with any values loaded from rustc (if enabled)

          Default is no features

      --load-cfg-from-rustc
          Load rust cfg values using `rustc --print cfg`

          values loaded are in addition to any values provided with `--cfg` or `--feature` respects the `RUSTFLAGS` environment variable

      --use-cargo-rustc
          Use `cargo rustc -- --print cfg` which allows automatically collecting profile flags and features

          WARNING: because `cargo rustc --print` is unstable this uses `cargo rustc -- --print` which may invoke side effects like downloading all crate dependencies

          (requires --load-cfg-from-rustc)

      --rustc-flags <RUSTC_FLAGS>
          flags to pass to rustc when loading cfg values

          (requires --load-cfg-from-rustc)

      --target <RUSTC_TARGET>
          A target to provide to `rustc` when loading config values

          (requires --load-cfg-from-rustc)

      --profile <CARGO_PROFILE>
          cargo profile to use when loading cfg values

          (requires --use-cargo-rustc)

      --package <CARGO_PACKAGE>
          cargo package to use when loading cfg values

          (requires --use-cargo-rustc)

      --no-default-features
          passes --no-default-features to cargo

          (requires --use-cargo-rustc)

      --all-features
          passes --all_features to cargo

          (requires --use-cargo-rustc)

  -i, --in-place
          When set, the generator will embed the common Zngur types into the generated header.

          The recommended workflow is to generate a `zngur.h` header with `make-zng-header` which should be shared between all of your generated modules and leave this flag unset.

      --cpp-namespace <CPP_NAMESPACE>
          Set the namespace of the generated C++ types. If not provided it defaults to `rust`

          You may use this to customize overarching namespace for all the generated types in C++, but if your project spans multiple .zng modules, you must use a shared namespace

      --crate-name <CRATE_NAME>
          Set the crate name to be used in the generated code where `crate` appears.

          If not provided, it tries to read `CARGO_PKG_NAME` and defaults to `crate` if unset.

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')
